module gunbc.recurring_failure_mode.a_confinement_witness_whose_only_writable_root_is_the_live_host

import std.types { NonEmptyStr }
import std.decl_ref { DeclarationRef, decl_ref }
import gunbc.recurring_failure_mode { BelowCeiling, FoundOnRung, RecurringFailureMode, RungRead }
import gunbc.guarantee_rung { MechanicallyPreventable }
import gunbc.rung_climb { ClimbableButUnbuilt }

// A CONFINEMENT WITNESS WHOSE ONLY WRITABLE ROOT IS THE LIVE HOST'S PRODUCTION ROOT IS EFFECTIVELY NEVER
// EXECUTED, AND MUTATES THE LIVE ROOT ON THE ONE HOST WHERE IT RUNS. Found 2026-11-01 on srv2, while
// bringing up the wet corpus there: nine install claims in
// `test.claim.roadmap.dispatch_worker_writable_sandbox_wet_witness` failed with
// `install: cannot create directory /opt/gunbc-lab: Permission denied`, and the spawn claims in
// `test.claim.roadmap.worker_git_subtree_apply_spawn_wet_witness` carried the same target. The cause is not
// the host and not the mode arithmetic: both witnesses built the instance under test with
// `roadmap_dashboard_instance.srv1_lab_dashboard_instance`, so `provision_parent_root` came from
// `srv1_dashboard_lab_provision_parent` = `/opt/gunbc-lab`, whose child `srv1_dashboard_lab_root` is the lab's
// instance root — the LIVE srv1 lab tree — and the claims' writes are aimed under that path. srv2 has user `briansrls` but no group `node` and no
// `gunbc-worker`/`gunbc-attempt`, so it cannot write there; srv1 can, and srv1 is the machine the lab serves.
//
// THE INVALID STATE, STATED AS A STATE AND NOT AS AN INCIDENT. A wet witness that (a) derives its install
// target from a production host layout, and (b) asserts a confinement boundary by writing under that target,
// has no host on which its evidence is both obtainable and harmless. On every other host it refuses at the
// first `install`/`mkdir`, so the guarantee its claims name is never executed — the corpus reads the file as
// enrolled (`gunbc.ci.ci_layer_roots` exclusion row + `v2.workflow.local_repo_wet_terminal` schedule row per
// function) and reports nothing, because nothing ran to fail. On the live host it passes, and the price of
// that PASS is writes into the root a production service serves from. There is no third case.
//
// THE HARM IS BOTH DIRECTIONS AT ONCE, AND ONLY THE SECOND ONE GETS ATTENTION. The direction people notice
// is a witness writing to a live root: the lab's `source`, `bin/gunbc`, `origin.git`, `attempts` and
// `attempt-state` trees are the lab's serving state, and a witness that installs into them has changed what
// the lab dispatches against — with no seal, no rollback and no receipt, because a witness's scratch tree is
// not a store it is allowed to own. The direction people do NOT notice is the one that actually applied here
// for the whole life of these files: the claims never ran, so a confinement guarantee that reads as tested (a
// registered wet claim, in the corpus, with a PASS-shaped name) had no execution behind it at all.
// gunbc.design_argument::fail_closed names this exact shape "a claim that cannot fail is a claim that was never executed".
//
// THE DISTINGUISHING FACTS — WHAT SEPARATES THIS STATE FROM AN ORDINARY RED WET CLAIM, AND WHY NOTHING IN THE
// PRESENT CORPUS SEPARATES THEM.
//
//   1. THE REFUSAL IS AT THE TARGET, NOT AT THE GUARANTEE. srv2's nine failures read
//      `install: cannot create directory /opt/gunbc-lab: Permission denied` — a refusal about where the
//      witness is allowed to exist, not about whether the sandbox confines. A red whose message names a
//      production path is not a red about the guarantee; and the same red, on the host that owns the path, is
//      a mutation.
//   2. THE TARGET IS NOT A LITERAL IN THE TEST, IT IS A PRODUCTION DERIVATION REACHED THROUGH A PRODUCTION
//      FUNCTION. `/opt/gunbc-lab` appears nowhere in either witness file; it arrives as the projection of
//      `srv1_lab_dashboard_instance()` → `isolated_dashboard_instance_admitting` →
//      `srv1_dashboard_lab_provision_parent`, and the field that carries it (`provision_parent_root`) is set
//      from a sibling field (`lab.provision_parent_root`) that a reader has no reason to suspect. A grep for
//      `/opt` under `dag/test` finds nothing, which is exactly how this survived review.
//   3. THE PRINCIPAL ARRIVES THE SAME WAY. `directory_owner` and `worker: WorkerDedicatedAccount` project the
//      srv1 account facts (uid 1000, group `node`, gid 1003 as measured there), so the witness also asserts
//      ownership against an account database that only srv1 has. An absent group makes `find -chgrp` exit 1
//      (`extdeps.tools.gnu_coreutils` says so of find 4.10.0), which is a second host-shaped refusal with the
//      same signature as an ordinary bug.
//   4. THE RUNG CHECK CANNOT SEE IT, BECAUSE THE RUNG CHECK ASKS THE WRONG QUESTION. The corpus knows this
//      file is registered and wet, and the schedule knows each function is named. Neither asks whether the
//      host running it can reach its target, nor whether the target is one the production model also hands to
//      a privileged writer — which is the same question the production layout in `gunbc.host.host_layout` asks
//      of a PRODUCTION write (is this a root the account owns), asked of a test instead.
//   5. THE FAILURE IS SILENT IN BOTH DIRECTIONS ON THE SAME FILE. The strongest version of this defect is the
//      one that never produced the srv2 reds at all: a witness whose target happens to be writable on the
//      runner passes, and its PASS is indistinguishable from the PASS it would print after mutating a live
//      root.
//
// WHAT WOULD REFUSE IT, AND WHAT WOULD NOT. A test of the derivation, or one more assertion inside the same
// witness, cannot: both are written by the party that chose the target, which is gunbc.design_argument::single_authority's point
// that a witness and its subject need separate sources of truth. What refuses is a check that reads the
// witness's declared install target from OUTSIDE it and asks two questions no participant in the witness
// answers: is the target host-supplied, and is it a path the production layout hands to a privileged writer.
//
// DISTINGUISHED FROM ITS THREE NEAREST NEIGHBOURS, because merging any of them loses a half of this one.
// `gunbc.recurring_failure_mode.a_plan_emits_a_privileged_argv_its_hosts_grants_do_not_authorize` is a
// PRODUCTION plan emitting an argv its hosts' grants do not authorize: there the emitter is production, the
// harm is a privileged action attempted, and the repair changes the plan. Here the emitter is a TEST, the harm
// runs the other way — the action is never attempted, or it is attempted against state that is serving traffic
// — and nothing in any production plan changes; the repair is where the witness gets its root. Merging them
// would let a test-only target walk out of the plan check and let a production privileged argv be dispositioned
// as a fixture problem. `gunbc.recurring_failure_mode.a_green_case_set_that_counts_row_identities_and_never_the
// _branches_those_rows_take` is a green verdict that is true about the WRONG OBJECT; its repair is to count the
// right thing. Here the verdict is about the right object and the host is what makes it uninformative — the
// same PASS comes out of a runner that could not reach the target and a host that just wrote into it. Merging
// them loses the mutation half, which is the half that makes this an emergency rather than a coverage gap.
// `gunbc.recurring_failure_mode.a_host_property_refusal_missing_from_the_infra_allowlist` is an observed,
// correctly-typed host refusal that was misfiled; its repair is the allowlist. THE ALLOWLIST IS THE TRAP HERE.
// Adding these nine `Permission denied` failures to an infra allowlist would close nine reds and leave the class
// exactly where it is, because that refusal is not about the infrastructure's own contract — it is production
// state saying it is not yours. A refusal is allowlistable when it is about the runner, and never when it is
// about the target.
//
// THE PAIRING OBLIGATION, WHICH IS WHY THIS ROW IS NOT "DELETE THE PRODUCTION DERIVATION". Dropping
// `srv1_lab_dashboard_instance()` from these witnesses would remove the last execution of the real path, so
// the repair keeps exactly one claim over it —
// `test.claim.roadmap.dashboard_lab_instance_inhabitance_witness` — which asserts the shape the live host
// actually gets (every root-derived path under the lab root, the owner, the group, the declared modes) as a
// PURE DERIVATION: no process, no file, no `install`. That claim is what makes deleting the real derivation
// fail a control, and it is the reason supplying a root is not the same act as deleting one.

data a_confinement_witness_whose_only_writable_root_is_the_live_host: RecurringFailureMode = RecurringFailureMode {
  identity: "a_confinement_witness_whose_only_writable_root_is_the_live_host" as NonEmptyStr,

  // FOUND ON THE LADDER, AT MITIGATABLE — and the mitigation is what hides it. Each function is named by a
  // `gunbc.ci.ci_layer_roots` exclusion row and a `v2.workflow.local_repo_wet_terminal` schedule row: the
  // corpus's own statement that this claim runs, carried by a human reading a schedule and remembering that a
  // wet claim has a host. What the schedule points at is a target the claim can only reach on the live host, so
  // the mitigation is what makes the absence of execution invisible — a file in the schedule is what everyone
  // reads as "run". The ceiling is MechanicallyPreventable: the two questions in the header are decidable by
  // reading the witness's declared target against the production layout, with no judgment about what the witness
  // is for, which is the definition of a boundary that closes mechanically rather than by a reviewer
  // remembering to ask.
  rung: RungRead {
    found_at: FoundOnRung { rung: Mitigatable },
    standing: BelowCeiling {
      current: FoundOnRung { rung: Mitigatable },
      ceiling: MechanicallyPreventable,
      blocker: ClimbableButUnbuilt,
      trigger_capability: "the capability to refuse a wet witness whose install target the production model also hands to a privileged writer: a corpus-side check that, for every registered wet claim, resolves each root the claim writes under to a value SUPPLIED BY THE CLAIM (an mktemp leaf under a scratch prefix, a `$TMPDIR` parent, or a host-said principal) rather than a projection of a production host layout, and refuses the row when any such root is reachable from a production provision parent — `srv1_dashboard_lab_provision_parent` and its siblings in `gunbc.host.host_layout` — the same way the production layout in `gunbc.host.host_layout` names the roots an account does not own, together with the schedule-side check that any witness needing an ungranted-versus-granted pair names a non-live host in its `v2.workflow.local_repo_wet_terminal` row. SUFFICIENT FOR: the srv2 red becomes a refusal at the schedule, naming the path, instead of nine install failures that read like a bug; and the srv1 PASS becomes impossible to obtain silently. INDIFFERENT to: whether the witness takes its root from mktemp, from `$TMPDIR`, or from a declared scratch prefix, and which account it nominates, so long as neither arrives through a production layout projection. It retires no claim: the confinement legs keep asserting uid, gid, octal modes, the writable worktree and the refusal outside it — they only stop inheriting WHERE from production."
    },
  },

  receipts: [
    // THE RED, AND THE OBSERVATION THAT FILED THIS ROW. Nine install claims in
    // `test.claim.roadmap.dispatch_worker_writable_sandbox_wet_witness` on srv2, 2026-11-01, all
    // `install: cannot create directory /opt/gunbc-lab: Permission denied`; the same target is carried by the
    // spawn claims in `test.claim.roadmap.worker_git_subtree_apply_spawn_wet_witness` through
    // `wet_lab_local_instance`, which copied `lab.provision_parent_root` and `lab.instance_root` while
    // overriding only `repo_root`. srv2 has user `briansrls`, no group `node`, no `gunbc-worker`, no
    // `gunbc-attempt`. Reported by jolly-hawk-60; operator relay stern-ram-763 approved the repair.
    "OBSERVED srv2 2026-11-01: 9 install claims of test.claim.roadmap.dispatch_worker_writable_sandbox_wet_witness fail `install: cannot create directory /opt/gunbc-lab: Permission denied`; srv2 carries no group `node` and neither gunbc-worker nor gunbc-attempt, and srv1 — the only host where those claims could write — is live.",
    // THE REPAIR, AND ITS RECEIPT — AND ITS SCOPE, WHICH IS PART OF THE RECEIPT. One of the two witnesses
    // named above has moved onto a supplied root and principal; the other has NOT, and is recorded as an open
    // case in the next receipt rather than folded into this one. A row that read as repaired while the nine
    // reds were still standing would be the same defect one level up.
    "REPAIRED BY PR #15627 (session zesty-stag-715) FOR ONE OF THE TWO WITNESSES: test.claim.roadmap.worker_git_subtree_apply_spawn_wet_witness now takes both its instance root and its principal from test.claim.roadmap.witness_supplied_instance — the root from an mktemp leaf under a declared scratch prefix, the principal from what the host SAYS via id/whoami — while the instance is still built through the production derivation (dashboard_instance_child over the supplied root) so only the ROOTS and the PRINCIPAL come from the witness; the live derivation keeps exactly one execution, as the pure-derivation claim test.claim.roadmap.dashboard_lab_instance_inhabitance_witness, which writes nothing.",
    // THE OPEN CASE, RECORDED RATHER THAN CLAIMED AWAY. This row was filed on nine reds in the SANDBOX witness;
    // PR #15627 moves the SPAWN witness, and the sandbox witness is held by another open PR, so the nine reds
    // are still standing at the time of this writing. Naming them keeps the row's rung honest (DESIGN section
    // 4b: the reported rung equals the rung established by executed evidence) and keeps the next session from
    // reading a closed row and walking past nine unexecuted confinement claims.
    "STILL OPEN AFTER PR #15627: test.claim.roadmap.dispatch_worker_writable_sandbox_wet_witness is NOT repaired by that PR. Ten of its claims still bind srv1_lab_dashboard_instance() — file lines 258, 290, 379, 481, 490, 500, 553, 580, 999, 1032, in writes_to_the_deployed_binary_and_tree_fail_inside_the_worker_sandbox, the_worker_worktree_is_writable_inside_the_same_sandbox, writes_through_the_launcher_to_bin_and_source_hooks_are_refused, sudo_n_true_fails_inside_the_launched_unit, sudo_n_touch_of_instance_bin_fails_inside_the_launched_unit, writes_through_the_launcher_to_the_worktree_worker_dir_and_git_objects_are_allowed, a_launched_unit_exit_record_names_the_expanded_invocation_and_result, a_wide_request_naming_a_root_exit_hook_is_refused_by_the_launcher, a_symlinked_request_body_is_refused_without_following, writing_the_gitfile_or_commondir_inside_the_unit_fails_and_a_worktree_commit_succeeds — so on srv2 its install claims still fail `install: cannot create directory /opt/gunbc-lab: Permission denied` and on srv1 they would still write into the live lab root. The file is no longer held: #15576 landed (023701ead322) and PR #15794 (session keen-crane-53) repairs the ten bindings in dag/test/claim/roadmap/dispatch_worker_writable_sandbox_wet_witness.dag. Read the REPAIRED receipt below for what that PR executes and what it does not: this row is BELOW its ceiling there too, and is not closed until the srv2 legs come back green.",
    // THE CONTROL, WHICH IS THE HALF OF THE REPAIR THAT MAKES IT A REPAIR. Pointing the supplied root back at
    // the live derivation, or dropping the supplied principal, makes the claims REFUSE — they do not fall back
    // to the production value.
    "CONTROL PR #15627: with the supplied root reverted to srv1_lab_dashboard_instance's own provision_parent_root/instance_root, and separately with the supplied principal dropped, the supplied-root witness refuses rather than running the claim at the production target; restoring the fixture returns the batch to green. Measured, not asserted: the revert turned the_spawn_grants_the_attempt_entry_and_refuses_to_start_the_unit_without_it and the_worktree_grant_makes_the_checkout_editable_and_its_proof_refuses_without_it RED, and dropping the principal requirement turned witness_supplied_principal_refuses_when_the_host_cannot_say and the_supplied_principal_is_the_hosts_own RED.",

    // REPAIRED FOR THIS FILE, NOT CLOSED. The live-row bindings are supplied-root rows now and both
    // controls are measured; the legs needing a systemd manager or a second principal are executed
    // nowhere yet, so the row stays open below its ceiling on this PR too.
    "REPAIRED FOR THIS FILE BY PR #15794 (session keen-crane-53): the ten `srv1_lab_dashboard_instance()` bindings in `dag/test/claim/roadmap/dispatch_worker_writable_sandbox_wet_witness.dag` are per-claim supplied instances -- each claim makes its own `mktemp -d` root, calls `witness_supplied_instance(scratch_parent:, repo:, principal:)` with the host-said principal, and is gated by the fixture's arms INCLUDING `witness_supplied_roots_are_off_the_live_host`, so a row that still points at a live root is refused before it writes; the helper `lab_instance_at_repo` (which took a root and grafted `instance_root`, the private git dir and `serve_binary` off the live row anyway) is deleted and its consumer now plants inside its own root. Two controls, both type-checking, both red on this container in one process whose thirteen untouched claims stayed PASS: one claim reverted to the live row goes FAIL, and a claim handed the deleted grafting builder instead of the production derivation goes FAIL. Correction to the receipt above: the tenth binding sat inside `lab_instance_at_repo`, consumed by `belt_commit_refuses_a_planted_commondir_without_running_git`, not in `a_symlinked_request_body_is_refused_without_following`. WHAT THIS ROW REMAINS OPEN FOR: this PR executes NEITHER the ten claims that launch a unit or exec the guest (this container has no `systemd-run`, one principal, and a 24 GiB memory cgroup that cannot hold the guest's second cold entry parse) NOR the two-principal DAC legs, which no single-account host can falsify -- those are QUEUED for srv2 (never srv1); they have been EXECUTED TWICE THERE (~/build/run15794d.log at 15be89fa, 3 PASS / 9 FAIL, and ~/build/run15794e.log at 54f3b519, 2 PASS / 10 FAIL) and every failure in both runs was this witness's own fixture or its launch route -- an absent attempt-unit spool tree, a credential still read from /etc/credstore, a source tree that was not a repository, status 218 from the `systemctl --user` route, an answer that never arrived, a cleanup that masked a verdict -- never a sandbox verdict, so the row is not closed until they come back green. LAUNCH ROUTE, stated so a reader does not assume the older shape: the nine unit-launching legs now go through production's own chain (attempt_system_unit_start_facts_for_request / attempt_system_unit_start_admitted / attempt_system_unit_plan_from_facts / attempt_system_unit_systemd_run_command), whose option head carries no RunUserManager arm and so targets the SYSTEM manager as production does (extdeps/systemd/systemd_run.dag:305-317, dissolution row systemd_run_manager_side_claim_gap, which records that no required automated claim covers that population); they previously launched through systemd_run_user_wait_command (`systemctl --user run`), a manager production never uses for this unit, whose status 218 (EXIT_CAPABILITIES) on systemd 255 was never a pass. Elevation to that manager is admitted only on a host that says `sudo -n true` exits 0, and elsewhere each leg refuses as 'needs a root launcher' rather than going green. Also still unguarded, unchanged here: `witness_supplied_instance` passes `provider_state.root` and `materialization_store_root` verbatim from the srv1 row and neither is in `witness_supplied_writable_roots`; nothing writes through either today, and the first claim that does must extend the guarded set in the fixture. DECLARED REDUCTION, stated rather than left to be discovered (DESIGN section 4b, no silent regression): before this PR these ten claims were DECLARED against the dedicated-account shape -- `srv1_lab_dashboard_instance()` carries `worker: WorkerDedicatedAccount` (`roadmap_dashboard_instance.dag:14`) -- and the file's own header required 'a user systemd manager (srv1)'; they are now declared against the service-user shape (`worker: WorkerIsServiceUser`), and because the guard REFUSES a live row, this witness cannot be pointed back at the dedicated shape without new fixture work. The bounded population that the dedicated variant alone supplies and that no claim here exercises: `UMask=0002` (`dispatch_worker_unit_confinement.dag:180`, with `data dispatch_dedicated_worker_umask` :179), the attempt-worktree `safe.directory` grant (:195), the operator route `LoadCredential=` (:405 over :393-395), the per-attempt private git entry's grant, recursive sweep and migration (`roadmap_dispatch_actuator.dag:2463`, :2533, :2562), the dedicated arm's attempt-state directory modes (:2342), and the worker identity spellings `fleet_posix_worker_user.name` and `fleet_posix_worker_shared_group_name` with their group-adoption and regroup policy (`roadmap_dashboard_instance.dag:1820`, :1829, :1836, :1852). This is a reduction of LATENT coverage, not of executed coverage, and the receipts are what say so: at the head this PR starts from, `src/v2/workflow/local_repo_wet_terminal.dag` carried ZERO `WetScheduledClaim` rows for the entry, so the wet lane skipped every claim in the file; the same pre-PR file contained ZERO assertions about UMask, `safe.directory`, `LoadCredential`, 02770/0664 modes or `sharedRepository`, so those six items were properties the unit under test would have carried, never claims this witness made; and the file's own header records that on srv1's shape the touch claim is RED (status 218 EXIT_CAPABILITIES, 'do not read its RED as the write succeeded'). The restoration trigger this row keeps open: a host carrying the `gunbc-worker` principal and the `gunbc-attempt` group, where a SUPPLIED row can carry `WorkerDedicatedAccount` and be handed a real per-attempt entry to be granted -- srv1 is the only such host and is CLOSED, and srv2 has neither today (`id` rc=1, `getent` rc=2).",
    "ROUTE, RECORDED WITH THE FIX (PR #15794, same session): the nine claims that launch a unit no longer launch through a user manager at all. Production starts a per-attempt unit on the SYSTEM manager — the property set carries no `RunUserManager` (`extdeps/systemd/systemd_run.dag:305-317`; the only arm spelling `--user` is at `:116`, projected at `:266`), and the dissolution row `gunbc.recurring_failure_mode.systemd_run_manager_side_claim_gap` is where the manager-side population is recorded. So the claims now go through `attempt_system_unit_start_facts_for_request`, `attempt_system_unit_start_admitted`, `attempt_system_unit_plan_from_facts` and `attempt_system_unit_systemd_run_command`, with `User=` from `dashboard_instance_worker_user` over the supplied row, elevated by the host's own sudo; on a host where `sudo -n true` is not rc=0 every one of them REFUSES with the cause `needs a root launcher` rather than passing. The observed `systemctl --user` exit status 218 (EXIT_CAPABILITIES, 16-18 ms in, with NoNewPrivileges, RestrictSUIDSGID, an empty CapabilityBoundingSet=, ProtectSystem=strict, ProtectHome=read-only and PrivateTmp on systemd 255) is therefore a fact about the USER manager under that property set and says nothing about production. Guests are served functions, not bare programs: production's admission refuses an `ExecStart` whose first word is not the instance's gunbc binary (attempt_system_unit_launch.dag:272-276), so the sandboxed touch, the two `sudo -n` legs, the exit-record leg and the worktree commit run as witness functions (`sandbox_touch_cli`, `sudo_n_true_cli`, `sudo_n_touch_cli`, `exit_success_cli`, `sandbox_worktree_commit_cli`) through `launcher_gunbc_run_inner_argv`, whose binary, source roots and entry are all accessors over the supplied row; a bare `/usr/bin/touch` was never admissible there, which is how srv2 run 15794e lost legs to an unreadable `test -e ... exit 1`. And because `shell.Exec.RunArgv` captures a child's stdout, the per-conjunct stage trail is written to a file (`data wet_stage_trail` = `/tmp/gunbc-sandbox-witness-stages.log`) rather than printed, so a failed leg names its own conjunct. Two duties come with a root-created transient unit and both are carried by symbols: each claim stops and `reset-failed` its unit BY EXACT NAME — the name production itself derived, `systemd_service_unit_name(stem: dispatch_attempt_unit_stem(node_id, attempt_key))` — and then reads the host's own unit list back, going RED if the name is still listed; and the attempt key carries the claim's `mktemp` leaf, so a probe can never name, and so can never mask, a real attempt unit. The distance that remains from production's own root side is named rather than smoothed: the static roster lookup in `dashboard_instance_for_instance_root` (`gunbc/roadmap/roadmap_dashboard_instance.dag:958-966`) is not exercised, because a supplied `mktemp` root is not on `public_builtin_dashboard_instances()` and the drain pass would answer nothing; the served binary comes from the planted file rather than the root-owned `attempt_system_unit_launcher_helpers_root` (`attempt_system_unit_launch.dag:93`); and one request entry is handled per invocation, so the drain loop's multi-entry semantics stay with the non-launching drain claims.",
    "THE PRE-LAUNCH GATE HAS A CONTROL THAT RUNS WHEREVER THE BATCH RUNS (PR #15794, same session): `test.claim.roadmap.dispatch_worker_writable_sandbox_wet_witness.the_supplied_plan_reaches_only_the_claim_root` (registered `WetScheduledClaim` / `ExpectedToHold`) asserts, without starting anything, that the plan production derives for a supplied row reaches only the claim's own root, that `User=` is `dashboard_instance_worker_user` over that row, and that the unit name carries the claim's leaf. It carries three NEGATIVE probes, because every plan this file can derive from its own row is contained and a gate replaced by `true` would sail through positives alone: a plan derived from ANOTHER claim's root must be refused, a property list with the `ReadWritePaths=` setting removed must be refused, and one declaring it twice must be refused. That last pair is what makes the writable-path arm, which is production's own equality claim `dispatch_worker_path_settings_are_the_set` (gunbc/roadmap/dispatch_worker_handed_set.dag:206) -- the path-carrying settings must be exactly the rendering of this plan's handed set, which a count of `ReadWritePaths=` settings would not have caught load-bearing rather than decorative. On a host with no systemd and no sudo — every host running this batch today — this is the only place those arms can be falsified, and the nine claims that need a launcher depend on them.",
    // THE THIRD EXECUTION OF THE NINE LAUNCH LEGS, AND WHAT IT ACTUALLY FOUND. The legs have now run
    // on srv2 three times (15be89fa, 54f3b519, fa64a4b9) and have produced NO sandbox verdict in any
    // of them, because each time something in front of the sandbox was wrong. This receipt records
    // the third cause and the repair, so the row is not read as "the launch legs failed" — they were
    // never reached, which is a different claim with a different remedy.
    "EXECUTED srv2 2026-11-05, PR #15794 at fa64a4b9 (stern; claim_batch sha256 f4a638cf…f854b, 'Finished release in 4m 16s'; logs ~/build/run15794f.outer.log and ~/build/run15794g.outer.log): 21 PASS in the batch — the nineteen scheduled claims and the unsandboxed control — plus the served-binary control run separately, and 9 FAIL, all nine root-launch legs failing with ONE cause and none of them a sandbox verdict. The stage trail reads root-launcher-available ok and spool-tree-prepared ok and then elevated-drain-ran with keys (bin, sudo-n-true, sudo-n-touch, wt, exitrec, roothook, gitfile) FAILED: the drain had run <claimroot>/srv1-lab-codex-feedback-v0/bin/gunbc run …, which answered `claim_batch: unknown argument: run`, because GUNBC_WET_SERVE_BINARY had been pointed at the batch runner and the witness planted THAT as the instance gunbc. So the fixture handed the unit a binary that cannot serve a .dag function, the unit's guest could not run, and the claims reported a missing marker. Two further faults the same run exposed, both about reporting rather than verdicts: twenty /tmp/gunbc-sandbox-* claim roots survived the run while the witness's own stage line called the removal housekeeping-not-verdict and read ok — the removal had been attempted and had not succeeded, and nothing read the outcome back — and `rm: cannot remove <claimroot>/source/.git: Directory not empty` appeared twice, also logged as housekeeping ok. Repaired in this PR, at the witness: (i) a served-probe pair in test.claim.roadmap.witness_served_probe (a module with one body that succeeds and one that refuses) is asked of the planted image through the SAME run --entry/--function route the launch legs use, and the image must answer both ways round, the refusal carrying its own body's words — the mode and the executable bit, which is all the served-binary readback used to check, went green on the batch runner; (ii) both root-side start routes now ask that question BEFORE anything reaches a manager and refuse with a named cause instead of enqueuing a unit whose guest cannot run; (iii) the cleanup readback is now a conjunct — `test -e` on the claim's own root, after the claim's own removal, an elevated retry and a settle — so a leftover root reddens the claim while verdict and housekeeping keep separate stage names; and (iv) the srv2 readbacks filter to --type=service,scope and record the host's own slices in a pre-read, because a bare grep gunbc also matches gunbc.slice, gunbc-attempt.slice, gunbc-attempt-worker.slice and gunbc-srv2*.slice, which are pre-existing and were being counted as if the run had created them. WHAT THIS ROW STILL DOES NOT CLAIM: the nine root-launch legs have produced a sandbox verdict on NO host — every one of the three srv2 executions stopped in front of the sandbox, and the legs are unscheduled here because this container has neither sudo nor systemd-run. The row stays open below its ceiling.",
    "EXECUTED AGAIN srv2 2026-11-05, PR #15794 at 114bcf31 (stern; claim_batch sha256 c6dae518…bab; ~/build/run15794k.log): 21 PASS / 9 FAIL — the nine again the root-launch legs, now dying ONE STEP BEFORE the sandbox. The drain ran the real CLI and the CLI REFUSED, correctly: the drain had assembled its own words from the claim's source copy (GUNBC_WORKSPACE_ROOT=<claim root>/source), and production answers \"workspace_root: ... names no release locus: missing member .gunbc-tree-receipt (the tree receipt the locus install writes last; a locus without one was interrupted)\" (src/v1/stage0/src/cli_run.rs) — a source tree is not an installed release locus. THE FIX IS A PRODUCTION CONSTRUCTION, NOT A FILE (same PR, follow-up round): the fixture now PRODUCES the drain's locus through the route that writes the receipt — `approval_broker_tree_install_at` (gunbc.exec.host_effect_realize), the receipt written LAST by `approval_broker_tree_receipt_write` after the per-file digests agree, under a claim-local base carrying the production step stem from `attempt_system_unit_launcher_locus` — plus a filesystem readback of exactly what the CLI checks (dag directory, .gunbc-tree-receipt, executable member). ONE argv shaper feeds the drain, the launch gate and the probe, so the drain's words are the probe's words. A host with no sudo REFUSES with a typed cause naming the elevation route (claim-locus-refused), never dying in the drain and never passing silently. The fault class is now LOCALLY DECIDABLE without any elevation: `test.claim.roadmap.dispatch_worker_writable_sandbox_wet_witness.the_drain_names_the_installed_locus_and_not_the_source_copy` asserts the drain's argv is the production locus's member, workspace root and source roots, never the source copy — it PASSes here with no systemd and no sudo, and the mutant restoring the source path is measured RED in the container. On srv2 the drain claims and the drain-shape probe go green, and the skip-install mutant is part of S1 so the fabricated-verdict class has a srv2 witness too. The rm \"Directory not empty\" print on <root>/source/.git is first-attempt stderr while the claim's own git-worktree children still hold .git; the retry's test -e is the verdict and a surviving root is the claim's own red conjunct. The ceiling holds unchanged: no host has yet produced a PASS with a sandbox conjunct on it.",
    // THE SIXTH EXECUTION OF S1, at 8948fed0 (stern, srv2, blanket sudo): NOT CLEAN on
    // every criterion, and it names the boundary that caused it. Receipt is evidence for
    // that head only; it says nothing about any head after it.
    "NOT CLEAN srv2 2026-11-07, PR #15794 at 8948fed0 (stern; logs ~/build/run15794q.outer.log, run15794m.log, run15794m-launch.log; identity asserted: fresh clone, `gunbc --version` = 8948fed0, script sha 3c2e0b51). host batch: timeout 124 -- the resolve alone took 682 s of a 1500 s budget, then 10 PASS, then `fold-cost-small` FAILed with `install: cannot stat '<root>/source/target/release/gunbc'` after 3.1M steps of setup, then the batch was killed inside `fold-cost-whole` (21 of 31 names with no verdict). launch batch: timeout 124 -- resolve 686 s, the freshness control PASSed, three legs FAILed with `the install fold converged but its receipt could not be sealed at <locus>/.gunbc-tree-receipt.sha256, so no leg may reuse this locus`, `launcher-refused` was killed, seven legs were never reached, and no unit-name line appears, so the stdout/attempt-identity fix was never exercised on that head. VERDICT IDENTITY: 42 names wanted, 28 missing. The seal cause, read from the code and not guessed: the locus and its receipt were installed through `sudo_elevate_command` (root-owned, mode 0444) while `witness_seal_locus_receipt` wrote the seal with the unprivileged write kernel-op -- EACCES on an elevating host, silently successful here because nothing is elevated here, which is also why no local run could see the defect. Each leg therefore ran its own install fold: about 27.47M eval steps, 668-913 s wall, no reuse. The plant also never staged the built binary where the install route reads it (`<repo>/target/release/gunbc`): that is why `fold-cost-small` died at file 0 there while every local run 'passed' it -- locally the install half never ran (430,047 steps, 320 ms). The freshness arm that deletes a claim FAILed there for the same seal boundary, since that claim reads the sealed locus. The script's post-batch tail died on an unbound stage trail under `set -u`, printed `(want 0, PASS)` regardless of the exit code for the freshness arms, and left two timeout-killed sandbox roots holding 0400 files named after the credstore key (0-byte placeholders made by `install /dev/null`) until stern removed them. What this receipt does NOT claim: no sandbox verdict for any launch leg; nothing about the two two-principal DAC legs; and the elevated cleanup was exercised under blanket `(ALL) NOPASSWD: ALL` only -- production sudoers would have to admit exactly `/usr/bin/sudo -n /bin/rm -rf <path under the run's sandbox base>` and `/usr/bin/sudo -n /usr/bin/install -m 0444 -- <stage> <destination>`, which is not established anywhere. The install-half cost shape (about 2,700 steps per planted file, against about 42 for the observe half over the same files) is recorded here as unresolved at this head, not as a budget input.",
    // AND THE ORDER THAT HID IT FROM EVERY LOCAL RUN NOW HAS ITS OWN CONTROL. Restoring
    // fold-before-receipt in the locus gate (mutant M8) turns the seal claim red on a host that cannot
    // elevate: FAIL, 5 stage records against 19 for the same claim clean, 0 closure diagnostics, measured
    // 2026-11-07. It is visible at all only because the claim now names the refusal REASON ID it expects
    // instead of accepting any refusal -- the old assertion (`does not read as installed`) was satisfied by
    // the elevation refusal, which is how this boundary stayed green locally at every head before it.
  ],

